section: compiler
synopsis: Add the experimental ``-unit-store ⟨dir⟩`` flag, a package database located by convention.
description:
  A unit store is a directory in which every installed unit is a subdirectory
  ``⟨unit-id⟩/`` holding a small ``unit.meta`` manifest and a ``lib/`` directory
  with the unit's interface files and ``libHS⟨unit-id⟩`` libraries, which is the
  layout ``cabal-install``'s store already uses. All paths are derived from the
  layout; the manifest carries only the module lists, the dependencies, the
  Backpack instantiation, a name/version label and the C-side link options.

  ``-unit-store ⟨dir⟩`` adds such a directory to the package database stack,
  and environment files accept a ``unit-store ⟨dir⟩`` directive. Units in a
  store are exposed and untrusted; visibility and trust are controlled by the
  usual flags.

  With ``-hide-all-packages`` and unit ids on every package flag, which is
  how Cabal and environment files drive GHC, the compiler now builds its
  unit state from the units named and their dependencies only: a unit store
  is not enumerated, package databases are still read but units outside
  that closure are not validated, do not appear in ``--show-packages`` and
  are not mentioned as hidden when a module is not found. The new
  ``-trust-id ⟨unit-id⟩`` and ``-distrust-id ⟨unit-id⟩`` flags are the
  by-id forms of ``-trust`` and ``-distrust``. Nothing changes for command
  lines that name a package by name.

  The ``ghc-internal`` and ``rts`` units are now registered under the unit
  ids they are compiled with, ``ghc-internal`` and ``rts``, instead of
  versioned ids, so the compiler no longer rewrites ("wires") unit ids when
  it reads a package database. The unit of the external interpreter is read
  from the settings file (``ghci unit-id``) and can be overridden with
  ``-ghci-unit-id ⟨unit-id⟩``.
